{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And the Fear of Isaac was for me.</b> He did not wish to say \"the God of Isaac\" because the Holy One, blessed be He, does not unify His name upon the righteous during their lifetimes. And even though He said to him when he went out (Genesis 28:13), \"I am YHWH, the God of Abraham your father and the God of Isaac\" — that was because his eyes had grown dim and he was accounted as though dead. Jacob was afraid to say it — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 31:42). And so is the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 31:42), for \"the Fear of Isaac\" — his God: \"דְּדָחֵיל לֵיהּ יִצְחָק\" (whom Isaac fears). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 31:42) that the fear of Isaac for HaShem availed me, for the merit of the father avails the son, as it says, \"And Jacob swore by the Fear of his father Isaac\" (Genesis 31:53) — by Him whom Isaac fears. Now, the interpretation is not the same in both. He further wrote (there), and there are those who say that this Fear is an allusion to the day of the Binding, and it is not far-fetched. And by way of truth, the expression will come in its plain and apparent sense, and it is the attribute of Justice on high, and from it Scripture said (Hosea 3:5), \"Afterward the children of Israel shall return and seek YHWH their Elohim and David their king, and they shall fear YHWH and His goodness in the latter days\" — it says that they shall seek the Mercy and the attribute of Justice below, and they shall bring the Fear of Isaac to HaShem and to His goodness that are mentioned:" "<b>And He reproved last night.</b> The language of reproof (תּוֹכָחָה) and not the language of proof (הוֹכָחָה) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 31:42). But it is more correct to take it as the language of proof (הוֹכָחָה), for he said above, \"and may He judge (וְיוֹכִיחוּ) between us both,\" and now he says that the Elohim who knows hidden things — He proved between the two of them, and therefore he did not say \"And He reproved you last night.\":"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The citation from Hosea 3:5 follows the verse's word order and wording in the Masoretic Text; Ramban reads 'וּפָחֲדוּ אֶל ה׳ וְאֶל טוּבוֹ' as linking back to 'פַּחַד יִצְחָק' in our verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:05.423367546Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}